one really exciting thing last year  about the Karen's Cup was that we kicked  off the event with an announcement that  the st. Louis chess club was partnering  with us chess to increase the number of  girls and women in the country who play  via generous program grant and I think  that just really ties in just so much  about what we're trying to do here at  the st. Louis chess club which is to  spread the mission of chess and  particularly to girls because we're so  outnumbered yeah and both as a player  now as a coach I already seen the  effects of it so as you know I believe  six top woman players receive grants for  our training those of us who are going  to qualify to Olympia's at least were  predicted to play in the Olympiad so we  have already received two grants so  that's has been quite helpful and as a  coach there have been several grants  that have been giving to different  programs that promote chess to girls and  I do work for an Academy with we don't  have a program like that but J Stallings  is a one of the best people I know this  Lea and he has a program in Southern  California so he has girls program as  well so he's used that money and we have  done already several sessions with girls  and it has been a lot of fun yeah I saw  that Jay is wonderful and there are  programs all over the country GC  Philadelphia my town Arizona as you  mentioned California we've been bringing  chess two girls in title one schools in  the Bay Area there's also a chess moms  program so it's not just about kids so  I'm really excited that everybody's kind  of on the same page that we need to get  better numbers for girls and women not  just for the sake of females but also  for the sake of the game itself like you  know from going to Olympia it's chess is  just a lot more fun when you have a  balanced gender ratio yeah and that's  what makes the Olympiad great and such a  great festival because there's just so  many people and it feels like everyone  is part of the game and everyone is  welcome there and I like too much as  mom's program because if you look around  and you see like so many like in  scholastic events ready city kids  playing and their parents just sitting  around and they spend the whole day  there and it's not something something I  didn't appreciate as a player when I was  young but now when I see it I just think  oh my god I can't imagine spending my  whole day sitting and watching my kid do  some  that I have no idea about so I think if  at least they know more about it they  can understand better what their kids go  through at least because when someone  loses a game I think only a chess player  can relate to that so I think if they  know more about the game how they can  relate better to their kids that's  really helpful I totally agree and I  think that's true for dads as well I  just think that the worst thing the  worst of both worlds is when the parent  doesn't know anything about chess but  somehow figures out how to get a chess  engine onto their phone watch their kids  came live and see what the engine says  about it it's like not an icon you got  to get in there and try to solve the  position for yourself so you can really  see how difficult it is for your kid  otherwise you're going to see some  parent who doesn't know how to play  chess ting why don't you play Knight h5  and I yeah I mean it must be frustrating  because you know you won the healthier  kid you wanna like maybe give them  feedback maybe the kid doesn't have a  code so you try to play that part but  yeah if you don't know anything about  chess I just can't even imagine what it  must feel like and one of the other  things we do at u.s. chess which is one  of my all-time favorite things is the  girls club which means that after the  games at a national events you can go  and get lessons from top female players  and meet other girls who play that's  really powerful for me because when I  was growing up there wasn't as many  girls who play